 * ---
 *
 * ## Branch Strategy
 *
 * @image html git-workflow.svg "Git Workflow" width=80%
 *
 * PicoKernel uses a two-trunk strategy:
 *
 * | Branch | Purpose | Protected |
 * |--------|---------|-----------|
 * | `main` | Stable, tagged releases only | ✅ Yes |
 * | `dev` | Integration branch - all feature work merges here | ✅ Yes |
 * | `feat/<layer>/<name>` | New feature development | No |
 * | `bugfix/<layer>/<name>` | Bug fixes | No |
 * | `docs/<name>` | Documentation-only changes | No |
 * | `hotfix/<name>` | Critical fixes to `main` | No |
 *
 * @warning **Never branch from `main` for regular work.** Branch from `dev`.
 * The only exception is `hotfix/*` branch.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Branch Naming Convention
 *
 * All branches include a **layer initial** to identify the affected subsystem:
 *
 * | Pattern | Use | Example |
 * |---------|-----|---------|
 * | `feat/k/<name>` | Kernel feature | `feat/k/ipc-primitives` |
 * | `feat/d/<name>` | Driver feature | `feat/d/spi-driver` |
 * | `feat/m/<name>` | Module feature | `feat/m/wifi-scan` |
 * | `feat/i/<name>` | Interface feature | `feat/i/serial-shell` |
 * | `bugfix/k/<name>` | Kernel bugfix | `bugfix/k/allocator-coalesc` |
 * | `bugfix/d/<name>` | Driver bugfix | `bugfix/d/uart-overflow` |
 * | `bugfix/m/<name>` | Module bugfix | `bugfix/m/nfc-timeout` |
 * | `bugfix/i/<name>` | Interface bugfix | `bugfix/i/shell-parse` |
 * | `docs/<name>` | Documentation only | `docs/arch-overview-update` |
 * | `hotfix/<name>` | Critical patch on `main` | `hotfix/panic-null-deref` |
 *
 * Layer initials: `k` = kernel, `d` = drivers, `m` = modules, `i` = interfaces.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Normal Development Flow
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * # 1. Start from dev
 * git checkout dev
 * git pull origin dev
 *
 * # 2. Create feature branch
 * git checkout -b feat/k/ipc-primitives
 *
 * # 3. Implement, commit with descriptive messages
 * # Stage changes interactively (review each hunk before committing)
 * git add -p
 * git commit -m "{layer-name}: meaningful commit message"
 *
 * # 4. Push and open PR targeting dev
 * git push origin feat/{layer-initial}/feature-name
 * @endcode
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Commit Message Format
 *
 * @code
 * <subsystem>: <short imperative summary>
 *
 * Optional longer explanation of why the change was made, not what it does.
 * What it does is visible in the diff.
 *
 * Refs: #{issue_number} #Optional but encouraged
 * @endcode
 *
 * **Examples:**
 * @code
 * kernel: add canary validation to kfree
 * scheduler: fix task starvation under high IPC load
 * docs: update architecture overview layering table
 * @endcode
 *
 * - Use the imperative mood: "add", "fix", "remove", "update"
 * - Keep the subject line under 72 characters
 * - Reference the issue number where applicable
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Merge Strategy
 *
 * | Scenario | Strategy | Notes |
 * |----------|----------|-------|
 * | `feat/*` → `dev` | Merge commit or squash | Squash for small / noisy histories |
 * | `bugfix/*` → `dev` | Merge commit | Preserve fix history |
 * | `dev` → `main` | Merge commit via `release/*` | Must go through release branch |
 * | `hotfix/*` → `main` | Merge commit | Immediately cherry-pick or merge to `dev` also |
 * | `docs/*` → `dev` | Merge commit | |
 *
 * @note Direct pushes to `main` and `dev` are not permitted.
 * All changes reach `main` via PR from `dev` only.
 * Hotfixes branch off `main` directly and merge back to both `main` and `dev`.*
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Release Flow
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * # 1. Ensure dev is clean and passing CI
 * git checkout dev
 * git pull origin dev
 *
 * # 2. Bump version in include/version.h (Read the note below first)
 * # PICOKERNEL_VERSION_STR = "0.3.0-alpha"
 * git add include/version.h
 * git commit -m "chore: bump version to 0.3.0-alpha"
 *
 * # 3. Merge dev to main
 * git checkout main
 * git merge --no-ff dev
 *
 * # 4. Tag and push
 * git tag -a v0.3.0-alpha -m "Release 0.3.0-alpha: M5 + M6 complete"
 * git push origin main --tags
 * @endcode
 *
 * @note The version bump action fires automatically on tag push and updates
 * `include/version.h`, `docs/Doxyfile`, and the README badge.
 * Manual bump in step 2 is only needed if the action has not run yet.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * ## Hotfix Flow
 *
 * Used only for critical bugs on a stable tagged release.
 *
 * @code{.sh}
 * # 1. Branch from main
 * git checkout main
 * git pull origin main
 * git checkout -b hotfix/panic-null-deref
 *
 * # 2. Fix, test, bump PATCH version
 *
 * # 3. Merge to main and tag
 * git checkout main
 * git merge --no-ff hotfix/panic-null-deref
 * git tag -a v1.0.1 -m "Hotfix: panic null deref on init"
 * git push origin main --tags
 *
 * # 4. Merge back to dev
 * git checkout dev
 * git merge --no-ff hotfix/panic-null-deref
 * git push origin dev
 * @endcode
 *
